  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nGloomy wet November weather continues for a third day today as\na sub-1000 mb low continues remains stalled out as it occludes \ndirectly over the area. The low will slowly move off to the east\ninto the Great Lakes overnight into tomorrow along with the \nlingering light rain that we've seen through out today. \nWidespread cloud cover will still stick around along with \ncooler temperatures as northwest flow keeps temperatures in the \n40s through the day tomorrow. Clouds will eventually clear out \nFriday morning as drier air filters into the region from the \nnorthwest. We could see widespread sub-freezing temperatures \nFriday morning if the clouds are able to clear out during the \novernight hours, but AI-guidance & the latest high-resilution \nguidance trends suggests we'll probably stay cloudy & warmer \nthrough the night.\n\nFriday starts an extended stretch of dry weather as a number of\nhigh pressure systems generally dominate the weather regime \nover the Upper Midwest & eastern CONUS as a whole. AI & physical\nensemble guidance suggests we have little chance of any \nappreciable rainfall over the next two weeks, with maybe a weak \nclipper giving us a quick hit of light rain for the middle of \nnext week. Temperatures will be above-normal through the period,\nwith the warmest weather coming early next week as upper-level \nridging builds over the central US & ensemble guidance shows \ntemperature anomalies of 10-15 F above normal. For context, \nnormal temperatures this time of November are in the the upper \n40s for highs & low 30s for lows. Temperatures dip a bit later \nin the week but return to well- above normal values next weekend\nthrough mid- November as pronounced ridging looks to rebuild \nover the central CONUS.",
